[Brain: Fireball-Project-Context]
[Invariants]
  □ Design Truth := docs/specifications (Natural Language is the source of Design Intent)
  □ Interface Truth := WIT (Canonical Interface Definition derived from Design Truth)
  □ ∀logic ∈ ComplexStateMachines : verified_by(logic, TLA+) (@see .agent/workflows/development_cycle.md)
  □ ∀c ∈ Components : is_tier(c, 2) ⇒ matches_contract(c, harness_pattern) ∧ is_pure_static(c) (@see docs/patterns/concept_harness.md)
  □ ∀f ∈ funs(Tier2) : ¬virtual(f) ∧ ¬override(f) (@see .agent/rules/embedded_cpp_rules.md)
  □ ∀m ∈ Allocations : ¬dynamic(m) ∧ is_heap_less(m) (@see .agent/skills/cpp_embedded/SKILL.md §メモリ管理パターン)

[Design-Patterns]
  - Harness (Tier 2): composition(TemplateInjection) ∧ cost(vtable) == 0 (@see docs/patterns/concept_harness.md)
  - Recovery: ∀f ∈ API : return_type(f) == result<T, recovery_strategy> (@see .agent/skills/cpp_embedded/SKILL.md §エラーハンドリング)
  - Layout: ThinGlueLayer(WIT, C++) ∧ FlatInterface(Components) (@see .agent/rules/general_design_rules.md §ThinGlueLayer, §FlatInterface)
  - vMMIO-Model: ALL host-guest data exchange goes through vMMIO.
      Physical registers (GPIO), shared memory, syscall buffers — everything.
      @rationale ONE mechanism for everything. No IPC for data, no separate pointer validation.
      @mechanism guest_access(addr) → permission_check(addr) → direct_physical_access(addr)
      @inv ∀access ∈ HostGuestBoundary : mediated_by(access) == vMMIO_permission_table
      @inv ∀addr : permitted(addr) ⇒ direct_access(addr); ¬permitted(addr) ⇒ reject(addr)
      @note vsoc_validate_ptr IS the permission check, not a separate mechanism.
            Security model has exactly ONE gate. No layering, no special cases.
      @paths PathA: guest load/store(vMMIO_addr) → permission_table → direct_access
             PathB: guest fireball_call(id, args) → host proxy → vMMIO → permission_table → direct_access
      @note fireball_call = vMMIO proxy for languages that cannot access physical addresses.
            It wraps ALL vMMIO functionality, not just specific operations.
      @layout PASSTHROUGH region: page 12288, 4096 pages = 256MB for physical address mapping
              physical_addr = passthrough_base + (guest_addr - passthrough_region_start)
              passthrough_base is in vm-config (ARM Cortex-M default: 0x4000_0000)
              Total vMMIO usage: ~1GB / 4GB wasm32 space → 3GB headroom
      @note 256MB > any realistic 32-bit MCU physical memory (Cortex-M7+SDRAM ≈ 32MB max).
            No new 32-bit processor will ship with 256MB+. This is effectively full coverage.
      @fallback wasm64 exists as theoretical escape hatch; not expected to be needed.

[Resolved-Frictions]
  - wit-syntax: ∀id ∈ WIT_Identifiers : ¬contains(id, "_") ∧ contains(id, "-")
  - cross-pkg: ∀r ∈ CrossPackageResources : type(r) == address

[Physical-Time-Model]
  @purpose Agent must understand: it operates in minutes, the human operates in days.
           The asymmetry is not a bottleneck — it is the architecture of this collaboration.

  - PROJECT_START := 2026-01-11
  - PHASE0_DEADLINE := 2026-03-31 (design phase, extended 2026/01/21)
  - PHASE1_DEADLINE := 2026-06-30 (vSoC implementation)
  - CURRENT_PHASE := Phase0 (design)
  - CURRENT_DATE := 2026-02-17
  - elapsed(Phase0) ≈ 37 days / 79 days total → 47% elapsed

  [Clock-Asymmetry]
    @inv agent_output_rate >> human_review_rate
    @inv approval_gate ≠ quality_ritual; approval_gate == rate_limiter(human_clock)
    - t(agent_draft) ≈ 5 min; t(human_judgment) ≈ 1-3 days
    - The human must cross-reference: physical constraints, team context, past failures, future risk.
      This cannot be parallelized. This cannot be accelerated. This is the most valuable processing.
    - Bonsai workflow is not just design philosophy — it is clock synchronization protocol
      between a MHz agent and a Hz human.

  [Observed-Cadence]
    - commit_interval: 1-3 days (not continuous; clustered work sessions)
    - active_hours: ~05:00-10:00 JST, ~14:00-18:00 JST (two daily windows)
    - burst_pattern: 3-5 commits/day when in refinement mode, then multi-day pause for thinking
    - The pauses are not idle time. The pauses are where design decisions crystallize.

  [Agent-Protocol]
    @pre before_pushing_output: estimate human_review_cost(output)
    @inv ¬rush_approval; wait is productive
    @inv when_blocked_on_human: do NOT pre-generate speculative alternatives to "fill time"
    - Deliver in digestible units, not in volume.
    - One well-formed question > three speculative drafts.
    - If schedule has slack, the human is using that slack to think. Do not consume it.

[State]
  - CURRENT_DATE := 2026-02-17
  - calendar_elapsed(Phase0) ≈ 0.47
  - maturity(vSoC_design) ≈ 0.8

  [Phase0-Gate-Criteria]
    @pre gate_pass(Phase0) requires ALL:
      1. detail_level(WIT) >= current + 2
         (interfaces need 2 more refinement passes toward implementation-ready granularity)
      2. detail_level(TLA+) >= current + 2
         (state machines need 2 more refinement passes toward verifiable completeness)
      3. verified(hypothesis(code_size, <= 15KLOC))
         (the size budget must be validated against the detailed WIT, not assumed)
      4. verified(hypothesis(performance, meets_target))
         (execution speed on target hardware must be credibly projected, not hoped)
    @inv maturity(0.8) means "shape is right"; gate requires "numbers are right"
    @note The remaining 0.2 is not polish — it is the hard evidence that the design works.

  @inv maturity(design) ≠ linear(calendar)
    - 0.0→0.8: divergent (rapid exploration, many options collapse fast)
    - 0.8→1.0: convergent (edge cases, integration points, the last 20% is the hardest 80%)
    - These two numbers do NOT imply "on schedule". Nobody said that.
  - brain_mode: MODAL_LOGIC
  - physical_time_awareness: calibrated
